{
 "result": "squeeze.py is built and verified: compress(bytes)->bytes and decompress(bytes)->bytes, stdlib only, scoring 5.0451 on the registered assay `python3 bench.py` (up from 4.6508 before the last rewrite). It writes one tag byte plus a payload and keeps the smallest of five candidates: stored, zlib, bz2, tuned raw LZMA2, or a token-level adaptive PPM model with a 64-bit range coder. The benchmark is a fixed, seeded corpus I generate myself: 300 KB of Zipf-weighted English-like text, 300 KB of JSON records, and a 120 KB mix of both; it prints total raw bytes over total compressed bytes for those three, or 0 if any round-trip fails. In the last run the text went 300123 -> 64164 (token PPM), the JSON 300048 -> 50276 (bz2), and the mix 120000 -> 28306 (bz2). Round-trip was also checked on empty input, a single byte, bytes(range(256)), 5000 random bytes (stored, 1 byte overhead), 100000 repeated 'a' (36 bytes) and a random 3-symbol string, all printing \"roundtrip ok\". The ratio describes this synthetic corpus only: the text is random word draws and I did not measure real prose or real JSON. The bench takes about 1.7 s. The PPM model did not win on the JSON or mixed items, so those are where further gain would come from.",
 "calls": 15,
 "secs": 1109.5
